{
 "id": "present-passive",
 "title": "Present Passive Indicative",
 "course": "Verbs · Passive Indicative",
 "intro": "The passive uses the same stems and time markers as the active. Only the personal endings change: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span> replace <span class=\"end\">-ō/-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span>. Where the active ends in -ō, the passive adds -r (amō → amor).<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch the 2nd person singular:</b> the 3rd conjugation has a short <b>e</b> before -ris (mitt<b>e</b>ris, cap<b>e</b>ris), not i. The ending -ris also has an alternate <b>-re</b> (amāre), which is not drilled here.</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Passive endings: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span>",
